From 37cd103b8a2efc9071b47c9b99fd08916bdcbbf0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] nvkms: HDR in YCbCr 4:2:2 at 12 bpc over HDMI where NVKMS
 would leave it in RGB at 8 bpc, when asked for

Where RGB cannot carry more than 8 bpc on an HDMI link, NVKMS sends HDR in
RGB at 8 bpc with dithering: on Turing, whose NVDisplay has no 10 bpc over
HDMI (supportsHDMI10BPC is FALSE for nvEvoC5); at 2160p50 and 2160p60 over
TMDS on any GPU, where RGB at 10 bpc needs 742.5 MHz of the 600 of HDMI 2.0
and FRL is not possible (a GPU without FRL, a dual-mode DisplayPort
connector, a display without it: nvHdmiIsFrlPossible()); and on a display
without deep colour. HDMI carries YCbCr 4:2:2 in the 24 bits per pixel of
RGB at 8 bpc, 12 bits per component at any depth (NVKMS counts it at the
pixel clock: GetMaxPossibleTmdsPixelClockKHz()), so such a link carries HDR
in 4:2:2 at 12 bpc at the clock it runs at now; the video is 4:2:0, so 4:2:2
keeps all of its chroma.

NVIDIA's own colour format request does not get there on Turing:
NV_CONNECTOR_COLOR_FORMAT YCbCr422 makes NVKMS choose 4:2:2 at the depth
that max_output_color_depth allows (10 unless set), and ChooseColorBpc()
refuses 10 bpc on an HDMI display of a GPU without 10 bpc over HDMI, so the
mode set fails (nvChooseCurrentColorFormatAndRangeEvo()); and nvidia-drm
gives that property to HDMI connectors only, not to the TMDS output of a
dual-mode DisplayPort connector, the HDMI port of some laptops. A client that
asked for 4:2:2 through it would also have to guess what NVKMS knows: whether
FRL is possible on the link, and the TMDS limits of the connector and of the
display.

So the choice is made in the mode set, from what NVKMS chose: a new parameter
of nvidia-modeset, hades_hdr_ycbcr422 (a bool, off by default, writable by
root, the name of the one of driver 580 with the same meaning), turns it on.
With it, after the timings are constructed as before, where the head signals
HDR (BT.2100 colorimetry), the client fixed neither the depth nor the colour
format, NVKMS chose RGB at 8 bpc without YCbCr 4:2:0 over TMDS, HDMI deep
colour is not turned off (hdmi_deepcolor) and the display is driven as HDMI
and declares 4:2:2 (NVKMS knows a depth of 4:2:2 for it), the timings are
constructed again in YCbCr 4:2:2 at 12 bpc (NVKMS_PIXEL_DEPTH_24_422),
limited range, with the format and the depth fixed; if that is refused, or
does not stay 4:2:2 over TMDS, they are constructed once more as NVKMS chose
them. The colour conversion (BT.2020 YCbCr for BT.2100), the range and the
AVI InfoFrame are NVKMS's own for that format; 4:2:2 sends no deep colour
GCP. hades_turing_12bpc (nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9012) is tried first, as
before: where it gives RGB at 12 bpc, nothing here applies.

Not touched: SDR (its colorimetry is not BT.2100), Dolby Vision (it fixes 8
bpc through "max bpc", and its colorimetry is the default one), a depth or a
colour format that the client fixed (the "HDR output depth" of 8 bits and the
"Colour format" of Kodi among them), YCbCr 4:2:0, FRL, DisplayPort, and every
head where NVKMS chose more than 8 bpc (RGB at 10 bpc over TMDS or FRL). With
the parameter off, the mode set is the one before, byte for byte.

The kernel log says what HDR goes out as where a rule of Hades chose it, when
that changes for a display: "Hades: HDR on <display> goes out in YCbCr 4:2:2
at 12 bpc: RGB would stay at 8 bpc over TMDS in this mode, and 4:2:2 runs at
the same clock", or that the mode was refused so and stays in RGB at 8 bpc.
The line of 9012 that HDR goes out at 8 bpc because the mode does not fit at
12 now comes only where 4:2:2 does not follow it. The decision is on plain
values in nvkms-hades.h for a host test.

Checked with a host test that builds nvGetHwModeTimings() and the helpers it
calls of nvkms-modeset.c, and GetMaxPossibleTmdsPixelClockKHz() of
nvkms-hdmi.c, as the tree has them, with stand-ins for NVKMS's construction of
the timings that keep its TMDS, FRL and depth rules: Turing with a display
that declares FRL at 23.976, 59.94 and 119.88 Hz, a display without FRL, a
GPU and a display with FRL, Ampere without FRL at 2160p60, a dual-mode
connector, hades_turing_12bpc with and without room for 12 bpc, Dolby Vision,
SDR, a format or a depth that the client fixed, RGB at 8 bpc over FRL, a
refused 4:2:2, and the log lines (407 checks); 25 mutations of the change,
each caught. The five modules build against Linux 7.3.4 with the compiler of
the image. Not run on a GPU.
